confirmed.correction:
The finding stands as written. Four corrections/sharpenings:

1. THE STRONGEST ARGUMENT IS MISSING, AND IT IS THAT THE SYSTEM ALREADY REFUSES THIS AT ITS OTHER DOOR. lib/live/setups.js validateOperational() enforces `typeof s.name !== 'string'`, so the direct rename door refuses the identical value:
   POST /api/live/setups/setup-mt39xner-076c61/config -d '{"name":{"a":1}}' -> {"error":"name: 1-80 chars"}
   POST same -d '{"name":12345}'                      -> {"error":"name: 1-80 chars"}
   relabel() launders the value through validName()'s String() FIRST, so by the time it reaches reg.updateSetup() it is a legal string and updateSetup's own type check cannot fire. This is not a missing check on a fresh field — it is one door bypassing the check the other door enforces on the same field of the same record. That is the case for the 400, not a taste argument.

2. THE REPORT MISSED THE WORSE HALF OF THE SAME HOLE: `why` on relabel. greenlightFromRun() explicitly refuses a non-string why (`typeof why !== 'string'`):
   POST /api/live/greenlight -d '{"runId":"...","name":"n","why":{"a":1}}'
     -> {"error":"a greenlight needs a WHY — record the reasoning that cleared it"}
   But relabel()'s why branch is `String(why == null ? '' : why).trim()` with no type check, and it overwrites:
   relabel {"why":{"a":1}} -> {"ok":true,"why":"[object Object]"}
   relabel {"why":[1,2,3]} -> {"ok":true,"why":"1,2,3"}
   relabel {"why":false}   -> {"ok":true,"why":"false"}
   Persisted record afterwards: "why": "false", with relabelHistory dutifully recording the real reasoning ("cleared on the money screen") as the value that was replaced. A name is a label; the why is the recorded justification for putting money on the config, the thing greenlight refuses to be created without. Same one-line fix (typeof check), and it belongs in the same finding.

3. TARGET IS NARROWER THAN CLAIMED, AND THE INTERESTING CASE IS A DIFFERENT ONE. Only a single-element array coerces through; the rest are refused by the anchor check:
   ["best"]            -> target "best"        (accepted)
   ["declared","best"] -> {"error":"greenlight target must be 'best', 'declared' or 'region'"}
   {}                  -> same refusal
   true                -> same refusal
   0                   -> target "declared"    (ACCEPTED)
   `0` is not coerced by String() at all — the route does `String(b.target || 'declared')`, so any falsy target (0, "", false, null) silently becomes 'declared'. That one deserves more attention than ["best"]: the anchor decides WHICH cell's numbers get frozen into the config, so a caller that sends no meaningful anchor gets a real one chosen for it rather than a refusal.

4. SEVERITY: agree it is above a 500, but state the reachability honestly — every one of these needs a hand-built request body; a text field on a screen sends a string. So this is a hardening/consistency defect on the money path, not something the owner trips over. What makes it worth fixing is (1): two doors onto one field, one type-checked and one not, with the unchecked one writing through the checked one.

