confirmed.correction:
Two corrections, one that lowers the severity of the headline and one that raises the precision of the expectation.

CORRECTION 1 — the armed-LIVE chain is not reachable over HTTP. The repro reaches `state: live` by calling `reg.createSetup` directly, an internal module API. There is no HTTP route that creates a setup from a caller-supplied configSnapshot: `grep -rn createSetup lib/ server.js` shows exactly one production caller, lib/live/greenlight.js:250 (the shuttle), and the shuttle's config is built by `configFromSelection` from a saved lab run's SELECTED row. So the two halves of the report do not join. I tested the join on my scratch server:

  POST /api/bracketlab {"set":{"geometry":"constructor",...},"windowLayout":"split70",...}
    -> {"batchId":"bracketlab-20260821-185135-real"}   (ACCEPTED — sanitiser bypassed, as claimed)
  final doc: status: done leaders: 0 edgeCensus: 0 runs: 0 failures: 3
  failures[0]: {"key":"LTCUSDT|BTCUSDT|ETHUSDT|constructor|directional|auto|24-7","error":"Cannot read properties of undefined (reading 'open')"}
  POST /api/bracketlab/<id>/select {"key":"LTCUSDT|BTCUSDT|ETHUSDT|constructor|directional|auto|24-7","stage":"slim"}
    -> {"error":"unknown leader row (promoted rows are the null candidates)"}
  POST /api/bracketlab/<id>/select {"key":"census|LTCUSDT|BTCUSDT|ETHUSDT|constructor|directional"}
    -> {"error":"setup not found in this run's census"}
  POST /api/live/greenlight {"runId":"<id>","target":"best",...}
    -> {"error":"this run has no selected row — select a leader in the lab first"}

So: real latent defect at an internal boundary, correctly diagnosed, screen-level consequences exactly as described IF a setup with that geometry ever exists — but "the setup is created, armed to LIVE" overstates what a user can currently do through the interface. It is one careless internal caller, one hand-edited data/live/setups/*.json, or one future route away from being live-reachable, not live-reachable today.

CORRECTION 2 — the second path's end state, which the adversary flagged as unconfirmed, is LOUD, not silent. The launch is genuinely accepted and `"set":{"geometry":"constructor"}` is stored verbatim in the run's params (confirmed above), so the bypass of the lib/batch.js:1169 sanitiser is real. But the run then fails every unit visibly: status 'done' with 0 leaders, 0 census rows, 0 runs and 3 entries in `failures` carrying the exact error string. Nothing plausible-but-wrong is produced there — the board is simply empty, which is the safe failure mode this project cares about. Rank that half low.

CORRECTION 3 — sharpen the expectation. The report says liveExecutable "exists precisely to refuse a geometry the executor cannot honestly run". Read literally that is wrong: liveExecutable (configschema.js:107-120) contains no geometry logic at all and never has — it checks cell.entry, cell.gate, trailMult, armMult, dMult only. But the report's instinct is right and there is a documented gap behind it: the doc comment on `liveGateErrors` (lib/live/setups.js, immediately above `const MUTABLE`) states the gate "answers 'may this setup honestly TRADE in state `to`?': the geometry must be one the executor implements, ..." — a check the code does not perform for any value. So the correct expectation is two-part: (a) fix the membership test at configschema.js:47 to `Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(GEOMETRIES, br.geometry)` (and the same idiom at lib/batch.js:1169, lib/dataset.js:173, lib/live/anatomy.js:19/46, lib/live/view.js:261/350), and (b) either implement the geometry check the liveGateErrors comment promises, or correct the comment — a gate documented to check something it never checks is the same class of defect as the truthy lookup.

